Parameter aliasing occurs when two or more formal reference parameters refer to the same underlying memory location in a function invocation.

This can cause unintended side effects when a write to one parameter unintentionally modifies the value read through another parameter. For example, consider:

void add_twice(int &acc, const int &val) {
    acc += val;
    acc += val;
}

If called as add_twice(x, x) where x = 5, the first addition doubles x to 10. Because val aliases acc, the second addition adds the updated val (10), resulting in 20 instead of the expected 15.

