This rule enforces the Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP) and guarantees compile-time type safety.

When a method is invoked polymorphically through a superclass reference, the Java compiler uses the superclass method declaration to determine which checked exceptions the call site must handle or rethrow in a `throws` clause:

```java
Base b = getSubclassInstance();
try {
    b.performAction(); // Base declares throws IOException
} catch (IOException e) { ... }
```

If the overriding subclass method were permitted to declare broader exceptions (e.g., `Exception`) or new checked exceptions (e.g., `SQLException`), the runtime execution could throw an unhandled checked exception that the call site never prepared to catch. Because checked exceptions are statically verified invariants, subclass methods may only throw the same checked exceptions, their subclasses, or fewer/no checked exceptions.

